WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:03.810
Hey youtubers, what’s up.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:06.069
This is going to be my Last Airbender Episode
1 and 2 video.

00:00:06.069 --> 00:00:08.980
The first two episodes were really designed
to be aired back to back so it just works

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:10.730
better if I do them in the same video.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:15.059
Hello to any new people, If you just found
me I’m re-watching all of last airbender

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:23.039
and doing videos for the episodes just like
I did for Korra.

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:26.220
And a weekly giveaway that I’ll explain
at the end of this.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.710
I’ll start by saying spoiler warning, just
in case you haven’t seen these first two

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:35.150
yet.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:36.450
TOP 5 Moments -

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.590
Number 5 - Katara’s Intro Sequence

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:42.410
I’m one of those people that only found
the series after it aired on TV.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.620
So by the time I saw it, I already knew who
Mae Whitman was.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.260
She had already done Johnny Bravo and bunch
of other voice over work.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.900
But she’s got one of those voices that’s
so much fun to listen to.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:55.070
Right now she’s doing the voice of April
O’Neil on Ninja Turtles, which is also airing

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:56.070
on Nickelodeon.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.740
I know some of you are fans of that.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:02.120
The Intro sequence also became a huge calling
card for the series in general, so much so

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.780
that they kept it when they produced Legend
of Korra.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.960
It works perfectly as an explainer for people
who have never seen the series before and

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.450
just looks damn beautiful.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:12.240
And I could spend all day listening to the
music score.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.670
If you saw the unaired pilot.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.170
They changed a lot and you can tell why.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:22.290
The final version of the intro speeds through
a lot of the exposition in the pilot.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.499
Explaining the 100 year war and differentiating
the characters.

00:01:24.499 --> 00:01:30.719
On top of that, the intro for the very first
episode Boy in the Iceberg, was extra long.

00:01:30.719 --> 00:01:35.549
They shortened the intro a little with subsequent
episodes just because they only have 22 minutes

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:36.600
to tell a story.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.340
So shorter intros mean more time with the
characters.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:43.700
And long intros get kind of repetitive after
a while.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.209
The era of the Netflix marathon watch has
made that pretty clear.

00:01:48.209 --> 00:01:51.850
Number 4 - Keeping Your Weirdness to Yourself
and Aang Gets Unfrozen

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:58.790
I like the way they connect the idea of expressing
yourself with bringing the Avatar back into

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:00.530
the world.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:07.179
If Katara had listened to Sokka and supressed
her bending there’s a good chance the Aang

00:02:07.179 --> 00:02:09.970
would have stayed frozen in the ice for who
knows how long.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:13.580
Bryke is trying to communicate that being
creative and putting yourself out there is

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.840
a good thing and you shouldn’t listen to
trolls.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.600
Represented in this scene by Sokka.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:22.640
Sokka did do a lot of Bender Trolling early
on but I never thought of him as an antagonist.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.680
I think up in his headspace he was trying
to deal with being the new man of the house

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.810
after their parents died, despite the fact
Katara was really the brains of that operation.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:38.970
Aang’s return was also one of the first
big action set pieces of Book 1.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:44.070
Every time I rewatch it I’m always surprised
by how quickly he jumps into casual conversation,

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:48.159
like it’s no big deal he just walked out
of a giant block of ice.

00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:52.989
I think they wanted to start the series off
on a postive upbeat note just to get you more

00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:54.890
excited for the hero.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:03:00.610
But if you think about the circumstances that
put him in the ice it’s a little inconsistent.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:05.180
He ran away after they told him he was the
new Avatar, flew into a storm and passed out

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.310
when he and Appa hit the water.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:09.730
So he would have been super scared.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:13.430
The natural reaction after being unfrozen
would be to scream in terror, because you’d

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:16.329
be locked into the original moment you passed
out.

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:20.000
So let’s just say TV Logic hand-waving.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.370
There was no way they were going to start
the series off on a bummer moment and freak

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:28.250
little kids out by having the hero scream
in terror the first time you meet him.

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:29.840
Number 3 - Zuko’s Intro

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:36.159
I wasn’t a huge fan of Book 1 Zuko but that’s
because Bryan and Mike did their job so well.

00:03:36.159 --> 00:03:39.320
You are supposed to not like Zuko, he’s
the antagonist of Book 1.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:42.409
Even Iroh is like “dude, chill out and have
some tea”.

00:03:42.409 --> 00:03:45.160
We’re on an extended vacation.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.260
You guys can let me know if you agree, but
don’t you feel like when a villain complains

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:52.959
too much or gets casually dismissed like Zuko
in this scene.

00:03:52.959 --> 00:03:56.120
You automatically stop thinking of him as
an insurmountable threat.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.700
He’s still a threat, at this point in the
series he’s not quite as good a bender as

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.109
Azula but he’s still really good.

00:04:02.109 --> 00:04:05.930
Little character quirks like this feel normal
for a children’s animated series.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:11.469
It’s like producers want kids to be afraid
without thinking things are completely hopeless.

00:04:11.469 --> 00:04:12.469
That comes later.

00:04:12.469 --> 00:04:23.210
Hopelessness comes later at the end of Book
2 in the crystal caves.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.260
Number 2 - Zuko versus the Water Tribe

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:30.289
I’m surprised Sokka didn’t paint his face
more later in the series when they knew they

00:04:30.289 --> 00:04:31.530
were walking into battles.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.259
He looks pretty badass with it on.

00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:36.120
And the stand off with Zuko was a great hero
moment for him.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:39.790
It was a lot easier to give Sokka big moments
like this before Toph came along.

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:43.580
It’s like the Justice League before Superman
and Batman get there.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:46.789
Once they show up, the benchmark for heroic
acts goes way up.

00:04:46.789 --> 00:04:53.210
That’s a whole other conversation, non-benders
in the avatar world can still have those big

00:04:53.210 --> 00:05:00.810
hero moments when they stand next to Aang
or Korra but usually it’s only when they

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:01.810
sacrifice themselves.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:07.120
When you think about it, there’s very little
risk to an Avatar getting into a fight when

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.090
they have nearly unlimited power.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:19.930
and 
my number 1 moment - Aang Escapes with Sokka

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:20.930
and Katara

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:31.840
This is the official beginning of Team Avatar
and the first big Aang versus Zuko fight.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.729
And the second big Avatar State moment.

00:05:34.729 --> 00:05:37.430
He was in the avatar state when he emerged
from the ice in episode 1.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:42.770
Bryan and Mike said that one of the big aspects
of the series Nickelodeon asked for was Action

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:43.770
without Violence.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.050
That’s where the idea of bending elements
came in.

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:50.230
So everyone wasn’t stabbing, slicing or
sawing each other in half.

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:52.430
Bloodless action.

00:05:52.430 --> 00:06:06.020
I’m always curious why more people would
get turned into fried chicken 

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.550
with all that fire.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:11.660
Here’s my question for you guys, what’s
your favorite moment from the first two episodes

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:15.023
and what did you think of the characters at
this point.

00:06:15.023 --> 00:06:17.680
Before you saw the rest of the series.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:20.259
I want to try something new with my Avatar
Episodes.

00:06:20.259 --> 00:06:22.990
Instead of just giving them a rating like
I normally would I’m going to do thumbs

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:24.840
up thumbs down.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:30.800
I might start doing top 5 thumbs up top 5
thumbs down from now on.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:31.800
Thumbs Down for What

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:35.129
I’m not going to be too hard on the way
the characters reacted to situations or the

00:06:35.129 --> 00:06:40.240
voice actors since this is a series made for
children and performed mostly by children.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:46.389
My only thumbs down are the lack of complexity
in the minor characters like the water tribe

00:06:46.389 --> 00:06:50.560
crowd scenes and inconsistency in Sokka’s
character.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.979
I feel like Sokka is undiagnosed bipolar.

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:57.830
At this point in the series, he’s a catch
all reaction gif for every twist in the plot.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:03.229
So I understand the role he serves and that’s
why I think he over-emotes and jumps from

00:07:03.229 --> 00:07:05.990
really happy to depressed in the span of a
few minutes.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:10.580
I don’t think that’s completely necessary
but it’s a cartoon inspired by anime so

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:16.740
I they were just doing their version of the
hyperbolic anime reaction.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:21.900
When the got to Korra the execution on those
types of visual gags was way better.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.000
I think it’s more a sign of the overseas
animation.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.890
If you’ve ever listened to the audio commentaries
on the Korra DVDs, Bryan and Mike talk a lot

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:37.380
about visual things they wanted to do on Last
Airbender that they just couldn’t get but

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:54.050
when they got to Korra, Studio Miir was totally
able to pull off what they asked 

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:57.310
for in terms of the animation.

